+# Frozen NDF descriptors + 3DFA: AWS Linux setup and training
+
+This implementation is based on **your latest `3dfa_se3-master.zip`**, not the earlier drawer package. It adds frozen pretrained Neural Descriptor Field features to the observation encoder. The first experiment is the single-arm PerAct **`push_buttons`** task. The same code can train other supported PerAct tasks by selecting different demonstration data.
+
+**Delivery status:** the source modifications and executable training/check scripts are included. Local Python/shell checks and launcher/configuration tests passed. PyTorch is unavailable in the development environment and its installation was blocked; tensor tests, pretrained weight loading, dependency resolution, GPU training and simulator evaluation have not been verified here. Run the included real-batch smoke check before a long AWS run. No pretrained weights or demonstration datasets are bundled.
+
+## 1. What changes, and what remains the same
+
+For each spatial visual token, compute an NDF descriptor at that token's existing 3D location. Concatenate the two features and project back to the original transformer width:
+
+```python
+# visual_features: (B, N, 120)
+# ndf_descriptors: (B, N, 2049), frozen, queried at corresponding token XYZ
+combined = torch.cat([visual_features, ndf_descriptors], dim=-1)  # (B, N, 2169)
+fused_features = fusion(combined)                              # (B, N, 120)
+# fusion = nn.Linear(2169, 120)
+```
+
+**The encoder's new fusion layer accepts the larger dimension. The transformer still uses 120 dimensions and 8 attention heads.** Do not change RGB input channels from 3 to 2169, change XYZ coordinates, or set the entire transformer's embedding dimension to 2169.
+
+This is feature concatenation at corresponding spatial locations. Unlike the earlier extra-token implementation in this conversation, it does not append more scene tokens. It keeps the original sequence length and positional coordinates. The updated visual features feed the existing proprioception context, density sampling, and action-transformer attention. Sampling choices can change because the features change, although the sampler algorithm is unchanged.
+
+The network flow is:
+
+1. Original RGB/CLIP/FPN and vision-language attention produce visual features.
+2. Original point-cloud resizing establishes the XYZ corresponding to each token.
+3. The frozen NDF network encodes sampled observed geometry and describes those XYZ queries.
+4. A trainable concatenation/projection layer produces the original-width features.
+5. Original 3DFA predicts actions with its original loss, optimizer settings and flow-matching procedure.
+
+NDF encoding and feature fusion occur once per observation forward pass, before the denoising loop. They are reused across all five denoising steps in that pass. No target action, task success label, or simulator ground-truth object pose is used to construct the descriptors.
+
+### Exact baseline settings
+
+The reference is `scripts/rlbench/train_peract.sh` in your uploaded ZIP, not a paper configuration or the generic defaults in `main.py`.
+
+| Setting | Uploaded baseline and new launcher |
+|---|---|
+| Dataset/cameras | `PeractTwoCam`: wrist and front |
+| Arms | 1 (`bimanual=false`) |
+| Batch size / validation batch | 16 / 16 per GPU |
+| GPUs by default | 1 |
+| Training iterations | 300000 |
+| Validation interval | 4000 iterations |
+| Data workers / cache / chunk | 1 / 0.5 GB per worker / 1 |
+| Learning rate / scheduler | 1e-4 / constant |
+| Backbone learning rate | 1e-6; backbone remains frozen |
+| Weight decay | 1e-10 |
+| AdamW betas | (0.9, 0.95), inherited from trainer |
+| Gradient clipping | 10.0, inherited from trainer |
+| Embedding / attention heads | 120 / 8 |
+| Vision-language / shared attention layers | 2 / 4 |
+| Proprioception history | 3 |
+| Scene subsampling factor | 4 |
+| Actions | absolute, quaternion XYZW, keyposes |
+| Workspace-bound buffer | 0.04 |
+| Flow model / denoising steps | rectified_flow / 5 |
+| Compile / EMA / lv2 batch size | false / false / 1 |
+| Training precision | original BF16 autocast and GradScaler |
+| Image augmentation, losses, normalizer | original implementations |
+
+The fusion layer is new trainable model capacity. Its learning rate and optimizer treatment use the original optimizer automatically. NDF computations use float32 with autocast disabled and no gradients; the existing policy computation retains the baseline's precision settings. Runtime and memory consumption will increase, so identical hyperparameters do not imply identical training speed.
+
+## 2. Identify your AWS hardware
+
+Run these commands **on your AWS Linux instance**, after connecting by SSH:
+
+```bash
+uname -m
+cat /etc/os-release
+nvidia-smi
+free -h
+df -h .
+```
+
+`xlarge` is a size, not a GPU model. For example, AWS lists NVIDIA T4 for `g4dn` and NVIDIA A10G for `g5`. This guide's installer commands assume **x86_64 Linux**, such as g4dn/g5, rather than ARM-based g5g. Record the full EC2 instance type, GPU name and driver version with your experiment.
+
+Use an instance image with a working NVIDIA driver. If `nvidia-smi` fails, resolve the driver/image configuration first; a Python CUDA package does not install the system driver. Use a persistent mounted disk for demonstrations and checkpoints. The supplied data downloader fetches the full packaged archive even when you train only one task.
+
+The uploaded trainer uses BF16 autocast. Do not silently change it to FP16 while claiming an exact baseline match. A T4 may need different precision support than an A10G; the real-batch smoke check is the practical test. If your instance cannot execute the baseline precision or fit batch 16 with NDF, either use suitable hardware or explicitly document a revised configuration and apply the same revision to the baseline comparison.
+
+## 3. Install Miniconda and unpack the code
+
+Skip Miniconda installation if `conda --version` already works. On x86_64 Linux:
+
+```bash
+mkdir -p "$HOME/installers"
+curl -fL https://repo.anaconda.com/miniconda/Miniconda3-latest-Linux-x86_64.sh \
+  -o "$HOME/installers/miniconda.sh"
+bash "$HOME/installers/miniconda.sh" -b -p "$HOME/miniconda3"
+source "$HOME/miniconda3/etc/profile.d/conda.sh"
+conda --version
+```
+
+Do not install over an existing `$HOME/miniconda3`; source that installation instead. In each new SSH session, source its `etc/profile.d/conda.sh` before activating the environment. Read and handle any channel terms prompt from conda according to your account/institution's policy.
+
+Upload the delivered ZIP to the instance, then:
+
+```bash
+unzip 3dfa_ndf_fusion.zip
+cd 3dfa_ndf_fusion
+conda env create -f environment-ndf.yml
+conda activate 3dfa-ndf
+python --version
+```
+
+The environment file creates Python 3.10 plus pip/setuptools/wheel. Everything below is run from this repository root. If you want to apply the changes to your own pristine checkout instead, see section 11.
+
+## 4. Install training dependencies
+
+For a compatible x86_64 NVIDIA machine, this is a concrete versioned starting environment:
+
+```bash
+python -m pip install --upgrade pip
+python -m pip install torch==2.5.1 torchvision==0.20.1 \
+  --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu121
+python -m pip install -r requirements-ndf.txt
+python -m pip install 'git+https://github.com/openai/CLIP.git'
+python -m pip install -e .
+python -m pip check
+```
+
+PyTorch publishes the 2.5.1 / torchvision 0.20.1 CUDA 12.1 pair. These versions are a compatibility recipe for this source, **not** a claim that the uploaded author environment used these exact packages or that this complete environment was tested here. The requirements file pins top-level packages; transitive dependencies and the CLIP Git revision are not a complete lockfile. New GPU architectures may need a newer matching PyTorch/torchvision build.
+
+Why these dependencies matter:
+
+- PyTorch/torchvision: policy, NDF and image backbone operations.
+- OpenAI CLIP: original RN50 visual backbone. An unrelated PyPI package named `clip` will not work.
+- transformers: original CLIP tokenizer/text encoder.
+- Zarr **2**, numcodecs and blosc: original stored demonstration format. The source uses `DirectoryStore`/`LRUStoreCache`, so do not substitute Zarr 3.
+- kornia: original augmentation; diffusers: noise-scheduler imports; einops: tensor layouts.
+- SciPy, tqdm and tensorboard: baseline utilities and logs.
+
+The two required official NDF model files are vendored with their MIT license. **Do not install all of `ndf_robot/requirements.txt`** into this environment: its robot simulation stack is unnecessary for frozen descriptor extraction and can introduce unrelated dependency constraints.
+
+Check CUDA and model dependencies:
+
+```bash
+python - <<'PY'
+import torch, torchvision, clip, transformers, zarr
+assert torch.cuda.is_available(), 'CUDA unavailable'
+print('torch:', torch.__version__, 'torchvision:', torchvision.__version__)
+print('GPU:', torch.cuda.get_device_name(0))
+print('CUDA wheel runtime:', torch.version.cuda)
+print('Reported BF16 support:', torch.cuda.is_bf16_supported())
+print('Zarr:', zarr.__version__)
+assert hasattr(clip, 'load'), 'Install OpenAI CLIP, not an unrelated clip package'
+PY
+```
+
+First policy construction downloads/caches the baseline CLIP RN50 visual model and `openai/clip-vit-base-patch32` text model/tokenizer. Allow outbound access or prepopulate those caches on your AWS machine.
+
+## 5. Download the pretrained NDF weights
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/rlbench/download_ndf_weights.sh
+ls -lh checkpoints/multi_category_weights.pth
+```
+
+The script uses the official URL from your uploaded NDF repository and leaves an existing weight file unchanged. If the link expires, obtain the official weights separately and update `configs/ndf.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "checkpoint": "../checkpoints/multi_category_weights.pth",
+  "latent_dim": 256,
+  "num_points": 512,
+  "query_chunk_size": 256,
+  "scaling": 10.0
+}
+```
+
+Checkpoint paths are resolved relative to the JSON file. The released model is loaded strictly. A missing/incompatible file raises an error; production training does not silently use random NDF weights.
+
+These settings are NDF-specific additions, not changes to the original policy hyperparameters. `num_points` bounds the NDF cloud input. `query_chunk_size` bounds decoder workspace; all visual-token queries are still described. The official scaling multiplies centered cloud/query coordinates by 10 internally; do not multiply them by 10 a second time.
+
+Run:
+
+```bash
+python -m tools.check_ndf --device cuda
+python -m unittest discover -s tests -v
+```
+
+The standalone check loads your actual pretrained file, checks descriptor dimensions and rigid-transform consistency on synthetic geometry, and verifies that the fusion receives gradients while NDF remains frozen. Unit tests use small random networks to test architecture; their success alone is not pretrained-weight validation.
