+## 6. Obtain and prepare push_buttons data
+
+The standard task in this uploaded PerAct code is **`push_buttons`**, canonical task ID **8**. This is not the separate single-button RLBench task called `push_button`. The PerAct task can include variations involving button sequences; using all its demonstrations is not the same as constructing a one-button-only benchmark. We retain the standard PerAct task here.
+
+If you have not downloaded the upstream data yet:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/rlbench/peract_datagen.sh push_buttons
+```
+
+This existing script downloads packaged demonstrations and clean test seeds, and creates `zarr_datasets/peract/{train,val}.zarr`. It is unchanged. It skips downloading directories that already exist; the converter skips Zarr outputs that already exist, so an existing output can contain earlier tasks.
+
+For a task-specific dataset with an unambiguous path, convert the downloaded `.dat` files with the original converter:
+
+```bash
+mkdir -p zarr_datasets/peract_push_buttons
+python -m data_processing.peract_to_zarr \
+  --root peract_raw/Peract_packaged/ \
+  --tgt zarr_datasets/peract_push_buttons/ \
+  --tasks push_buttons
+
+python -m tools.check_data \
+  --dataset-dir zarr_datasets/peract_push_buttons \
+  --tasks push_buttons
+```
+
+The trailing slash on `--root` is required by the original converter's string concatenation. Only load trusted `.dat` demonstrations: the original converter uses pickle. Keep canonical task IDs; the supplied converter already preserves them for subsets. Do not renumber the selected task to zero.
+
+Expected training data:
+
+| Array | Shape / meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `rgb` | `(N,4,3,256,256)`, uint8 |
+| `pcd` | `(N,4,3,256,256)`, world XYZ in metres |
+| `proprioception` | `(N,3,1,8)` |
+| `action` | `(N,1,1,8)` |
+| `task_id`, `variation` | `(N,)` |
+
+Camera storage order is left shoulder, right shoulder, wrist, front; the original `PeractTwoCam` dataset selects the final two. Pose values are XYZ, quaternion XYZW, gripper state. Instruction strings come from `instructions/peract/instructions.json`.
+
+If you only have raw RLBench images and pickles, they are **not** direct inputs to this `.dat` converter. Use the provided prepackaged dataset workflow or supply an additional raw-to-packaged conversion step. Keep train/validation/test episodes separate; this change does not generate new splits or labels.
+
+## 7. Run one real training step before the full experiment
+
+```bash
+CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 TASK=push_buttons SMOKE_ONLY=1 \
+  bash scripts/rlbench/train_peract_ndf.sh
+```
+
+This uses the actual dataset, baseline preprocessor/augmentation, original optimizer settings, batch 16, and the real policy. It:
+
+1. Loads pretrained NDF and the baseline CLIP assets.
+2. Computes training action bounds as the baseline does.
+3. Runs one forward/backward/optimizer step.
+4. Checks that every NDF state tensor (including buffers) stayed unchanged.
+5. Checks that NDF parameters are excluded from the optimizer and the fusion weights updated.
+6. Runs inference and checks for finite single-arm 8D actions.
+
+The diagnostic does not resume or overwrite policy checkpoints; its logs use the experiment's `smoke` subdirectory. Passing it is required before treating the implementation as runtime-validated on your machine. A CUDA out-of-memory error here means the complete batch-16 configuration has not fit that GPU; a baseline fitting does not guarantee the augmented model fits.
+
+NDF is processed one observation at a time and queries are chunked to limit temporary memory. These loops do **not** change the policy training batch, gradient accumulation, number of action samples or number of spatial tokens. Changing query chunk size is a workspace tradeoff; changing the policy batch is a training change.
+
+## 8. Train with the uploaded baseline parameters
+
+```bash
+CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 TASK=push_buttons \
+  bash scripts/rlbench/train_peract_ndf.sh
+```
+
+The new launcher is derived from your uploaded `train_peract.sh`. An automated test records both launchers' actual arguments and confirms that all training settings match, except dataset/output paths and the new NDF config argument. The original launcher is still present and unchanged.
+
+Default checkpoints are under:
+
+```text
+train_logs/Peract_push_buttons_ndf/
+  denoise3d-PeractTwoCam-C120-B16-lr1e-4-constant-H3-rectified_flow/
+    run_config.json
+    best.pth
+    last.pth
+```
+
+The first checkpoint is saved after validation at step 4000, as in the baseline. A very short `TRAIN_ITERS=20` run would not produce a checkpoint; use the dedicated smoke command for debugging. The default 300000-step run ends at a validation boundary. `best.pth` uses the original validation positional-accuracy metric, not online task success.
+
+The launcher automatically resumes `last.pth` in the selected experiment directory. Keep the same task data and NDF numerical config on resume. The fusion model cannot resume a baseline checkpoint as though it were the same architecture; use a fresh experiment directory. NDF weights are included in saved policy checkpoints, so resume/evaluation can restore them even if the original NDF weight-file path has moved. The configuration JSON is still needed to construct a training run.
+
+`run_config.json` records resolved settings. Also save your environment and downloaded weight hash:
+
+```bash
+mkdir -p experiment_records
+conda env export > experiment_records/environment.yml
+python -m pip freeze > experiment_records/pip-freeze.txt
+sha256sum checkpoints/multi_category_weights.pth > experiment_records/ndf-weight-sha256.txt
+nvidia-smi > experiment_records/gpu.txt
+```
+
+You may use `tmux` to keep training alive after SSH disconnects. To monitor TensorBoard without opening a public service:
+
+```bash
+python -m tensorboard.main --logdir train_logs/Peract_push_buttons_ndf --host 127.0.0.1 --port 6006
+```
+
+From your local machine, establish an SSH tunnel with your normal EC2 username/key/hostname, for example `ssh -L 6006:localhost:6006 ubuntu@YOUR_EC2_HOST`; open `http://localhost:6006` locally. Supply your normal `-i` key option if needed.
+
+### Baseline comparison
+
+```bash
+CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 TASK=push_buttons MODE=baseline \
+  bash scripts/rlbench/train_peract_ndf.sh
+```
+
+This omits NDF entirely and writes to `Peract_push_buttons_baseline`. Use the same data, training steps, hardware and precision. The uploaded trainer does not fully seed all RNGs; this delivery does not change that behavior. A single-run difference is not evidence of a reliable gain: run repeated experiments and record the initialization/reproducibility strategy if extending the baseline for controlled comparisons.
