+## 11. Exact source changes and how to apply them manually
+
+The supplied ZIP is already modified. Do **not** apply the patch again to it.
+
+For a pristine extraction of exactly your uploaded baseline:
+
+```bash
+cd /path/to/pristine/3dfa_se3-master
+# Keep the delivered patch outside this checkout.
+git apply --check /path/to/ndf_fusion.patch
+git apply /path/to/ndf_fusion.patch
+```
+
+The patch is in `docs/ndf_fusion.patch`; it contains all new source, configuration and guide files except itself and the generated source manifest. It does not delete files. You can also follow these edits directly:
+
+1. Add `modeling/ndf/fusion.py`, `config.py` and the two official vendor model files. The full implementation is supplied, not pseudocode.
+2. In `modeling/encoder/multimodal/encoder3d.py`, accept `ndf_config`/`ndf_load_pretrained`, construct `self.ndf_fusion`, retain `source_pcd` before resizing, and fuse immediately after computing aligned token XYZ in `encode_clip`. The base encoder then consumes the fused features automatically.
+3. In `modeling/policy/denoise_actor_3d.py`, pass those optional settings to `Encoder`. No decoder or output-head dimensions change.
+4. In `main.py`, add the optional `--ndf_config` JSON path. Omitting it leaves the baseline model architecture intact.
+5. In `utils/trainers/base.py`, parse/validate the NDF config, pass it into model construction, record configs in checkpoints, and check compatibility at resume. The existing optimizer already skips `requires_grad=False` parameters, so its grouping logic is unchanged. A small `checkpoint is None` guard also permits the standalone smoke check.
+6. In `online_evaluation_rlbench/evaluate_policy.py`, reconstruct architecture and NDF from checkpoint metadata, strip only a real DDP `module.` prefix, and select cameras after loading the metadata.
+7. Use the added `scripts/rlbench/train_peract_ndf.sh` rather than modifying the original baseline launcher.
+
+Frozen means all three of these are implemented:
+
+```python
+self.field.requires_grad_(False)  # no trainable NDF parameters
+self.field.eval()                # no updating batch-normalization statistics
+# Descriptor extraction runs inside torch.no_grad().
+# The fusion Linear layer is OUTSIDE that block and remains trainable.
+```
+
+The custom `train()` override puts the field back in eval mode whenever the parent policy is switched to training. Frozen weights alone would not protect running buffers. `fusion.weight` is initialized to `[I, 0]` and its bias to zero, so the added layer starts by passing through the original visual features; its descriptor columns receive gradients from the first training step and can learn to use NDF features.
+
+## 12. Interpretation and limitations
+
+- The original NDF network uses a Vector Neuron encoder and an occupancy decoder. The `acts='all'` descriptor has dimension `(2*256+1) + 6*256 = 2049`; it is not the 256-dimensional latent code itself.
+- One common sampled-cloud centroid is subtracted from both NDF input points and query points. The original world-space XYZ for 3DFA positional encoding is unchanged.
+- The default conditions on the observed scene cloud. The packaged dataset has no object segmentation masks; this is not an object-segmented NDF reproduction. Sampling uses fixed pixel-order indices, which are consistent under a coordinate relabeling but not arbitrary point permutation or re-rendering.
+- The released multi-category checkpoint was trained on mug, bowl and bottle geometry. Button scenes are out of that training domain. This is a transfer experiment; pretrained descriptors are not guaranteed to help buttons.
+- Frozen NDF plus an unconstrained policy does not guarantee end-to-end SE(3) equivariance. Success rate and transformation consistency are empirical questions.
+- Pretrained weights, data downloads, GPU memory and full training remain to be validated on your AWS instance. Tests and scripts expose these checks; no training results are claimed in this delivery.
